# PerfectMarkD API configuration — copy to .env (never committed) and fill in.
# In Docker/Compose these are passed as environment variables instead.
#
# ── Required ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Compose refuses to start without these four, each with its own error naming
# the key, so a missing one is never silent:
#
#   SESSION_SECRET          signs session cookies
#   HISTORY_ENCRYPTION_KEY  encrypts Premium Export History at rest
#   UMAMI_APP_SECRET        signs Umami's own login tokens
#   UMAMI_DB_PASSWORD       Postgres password for the analytics database
#
# Generate all four at once — they need no particular format, only length:
#
#   for k in SESSION_SECRET HISTORY_ENCRYPTION_KEY UMAMI_APP_SECRET \
#            UMAMI_DB_PASSWORD; do echo "$k=$(openssl rand -hex 32)"; done
#
# Paste the four output lines into this file. ADMIN_EMAIL and AI_API_KEY below
# are also worth setting at deploy time even though only the first four are
# mandatory.

# Port the API listens on.
PORT=3000

# SQLite database file; the parent directory is created if missing.
DB_PATH=./data/perfectmarkd.db

# Secret used to sign session cookies. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
SESSION_SECRET=

# First registered account with this email becomes the Admin (bootstrap).
# Register with exactly this address, or nothing gets the flag. The bootstrap
# only runs while no such user exists; afterwards this key changes nothing and
# the flag lives on the user record.
ADMIN_EMAIL=

# Server Export (server/03): origin the worker loads /export from — the web
# dev server in development (default http://localhost:3000, this API's own
# port; docker compose sets it to http://caddy, a loopback origin on the
# compose network that serves the SPA regardless of public TLS).
#EXPORT_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# Simultaneous export renders. Each one is a headless Chromium, so lower it on
# a host with little free memory — a 300-page document is not a small render,
# and two of them in flight is the default.
#EXPORT_CONCURRENCY=2

# Max exports one user may enqueue per rolling minute.
#EXPORT_BURST_PER_MINUTE=10

# Deadline for one Server Export job in milliseconds — the whole render,
# print included, before the job fails as render_timeout. A 300-page document
# renders in a few seconds; a minute is a stuck job, not a slow one.
#EXPORT_RENDER_TIMEOUT_MS=60000

# Export History (server/05): where finished Premium users' Server Exports
# are kept, encrypted at rest. Must be outside any web root. Required — the
# API refuses to boot without it, because Premium users' exports are
# supposed to be retained.
HISTORY_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Root directory for the encrypted history files (default ./data/history).
#HISTORY_DIR=./data/history

# AI Actions (ADR-0008): the provider's API key. It belongs to the deployment
# and is read from the environment only — never written to the settings table,
# never returned by an endpoint, never logged. Rotating it needs a restart.
# Leave unset for no AI: a fresh instance reports AI as unavailable until a
# key is present, and the Admin panel only ever reports whether one is set.
#
# This is the ONLY part of the AI feature configured here. The endpoint, the
# model, the reasoning effort, the token caps and the kill switch are all
# settings, edited in /admin → Settings → AI provider and stored in the
# database — deliberately, so a model can be changed without a redeploy. So the
# order is: put the key here, `docker compose up -d api` to pick it up, then
# configure the provider in the panel. The feature stays absent until both
# halves are in place.
#AI_API_KEY=

# Compose (server/06): address Caddy serves the site on. ":80" is plain HTTP,
# which is what the shipped deployment wants — a Cloudflare Tunnel terminates
# TLS in front of Caddy (ADR-0010). A domain gives automatic TLS via Let's
# Encrypt, which is only right for a directly-exposed host (ADR-0010).
#SITE_ADDRESS=:80

# Analytics (launch/01): the self-hosted Umami instance Compose runs at
# /analytics. Two secrets, both required, both generated with
# `openssl rand -hex 32`: APP_SECRET signs Umami's own login tokens, and
# UMAMI_DB_PASSWORD is the Postgres password for its database. The database is
# only reachable on the compose network and holds nothing but statistics, but
# it is still a credential — keep it out of the repo like the others.
UMAMI_APP_SECRET=
UMAMI_DB_PASSWORD=

# The Umami website this instance reports to. Until you set it, the app loads no
# tracker at all and collects nothing. To get one: bring the stack up, open
# https://<domain>/analytics, sign in (admin / umami — change it), add a
# website for your domain, and copy its id here — the bare id, as Umami shows
# it, with no quotes or wrapping. Caddy hands it to the app at runtime, so this
# is a restart rather than a rebuild:
#   docker compose up -d caddy
ANALYTICS_WEBSITE_ID=

# Where the tracker lives. Baked into the web bundle at build time, so changing
# it needs a rebuild. Only change it if you serve Umami somewhere other than
# this origin's /analytics — the Caddyfile and the Umami image both assume that
# path.
#VITE_ANALYTICS_URL=/analytics

# The Umami image tag Compose pulls from GHCR. It is built by
# .github/workflows/umami-image.yml; keep it in sync with UMAMI_VERSION in
# ops/umami/Dockerfile when bumping Umami.
#UMAMI_VERSION=3.4.0

# Host ports published by Caddy. Override only when the low ports are taken
# or unavailable (rootless container daemons cannot bind below 1024). A host
# already serving something on :80 — a reverse proxy, another stack — is the
# usual reason, and behind a Cloudflare Tunnel this is the only port that has
# to be reachable: the tunnel publishes this one, and nothing else.
#CADDY_HTTP_PORT=80
#CADDY_HTTPS_PORT=443

# Which published app images to run (launch/10). `latest` follows every push to
# main; a `sha-…` tag pins the exact api/caddy pair the workflow built, which is
# what a reproducible deploy wants. It matters when the images are pulled — a
# dev checkout that builds tags its own build with this ref.
#PERFECTMARKD_IMAGE_TAG=latest

# Backups (launch/03, launch/12). Off unless switched on: with anything other
# than 1 here, ops/backup.sh exits 0 having done nothing, so the timer can be
# installed before you decide, and nothing is forced on you. With backups off
# there is no recovery path — no dump, no history mirror, nothing to restore
# from (docs/ops/restore.md says the same at more length).
#BACKUP_ENABLED=0

# rclone remote:path the nightly job uploads to, e.g. "b2:perfectmarkd-backups"
# once `rclone config` has the remote. A plain absolute path also works (used by
# the restore rehearsal in docs/ops/restore.md). Optional: leave it unset and
# the backup stays on this host — a verified dump plus a history mirror under
# BACKUP_STAGE, nothing uploaded. Nothing then survives losing the machine.
#BACKUP_REMOTE=b2:perfectmarkd-backups

# Where the nightly job stages dumps before upload (default below). Also the
# whole backup in local-only mode.
#BACKUP_STAGE=/var/backups/perfectmarkd
